  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2ee87e681215b6147a44021cf30db039, type: 3}
  m_Name: 3.Nix Mangiare steps
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  nameOfPoint: Nix Mangiare steps
  coordinatesPoint: {x: 14.51472, y: 35.89614}
  firstPartInfo: "These days, I can walk up these stairs at my leisure, but in Isidoro
    Muscat\u2019s time, things would have been very different. Isidoro and his brother
    were far from the only ones burdened with poverty.   At the time, this flight
    of stairs was notorious. Those who could, tried to avoid it. Why? What awaited
    those who took these steps?  Here, poverty would not be ignored and could not
    be unseen. People begged all around, crowding visitors, for a crumb to eat, or
    a coin to add to their meagre provisions. \u201CCartit\xE0 nix mangiare \u2013
    nix padre \u2013 nix madre \u2013 nix pane per i piccoli in casa\u201D. (Adolphus
    Slade quoting the cries he heard; quoted by Giovanni Bonello, https://timesofmalta.com/articles/view/Appalling-poverty-in-Malta-in-the-early-19th-century.475512 
    Trans.: \u201CCharity, nothing to eat - no father - no mother - no bread for
    the little ones at home\u201D "
  secondPartInfo: "There was a yawning chasm between the rich and the poor; and Bigelow
    recognises this when comparing the \u2018troubles\u2019 faced by each class:
    \u201CWhy talk of petty inconveniences and magnify them into troubles, when there
    is perfect misery?\u201D (Bigelow quoted in Bonello)  I am aware that my route
    lies away from the extreme poverty to the affluence of authority which would
    no doubt have awed the young servant. To follow Isidoro Muscat\u2019s path, I
    must head to the heart of the city - to the palace from where its rulers wielded
    their power. "
  imgMarker: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c98feaebd40d8df449e6d3855ac84352, type: 3}
  imgMain: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f26fa8880b3aeef4b8516c82dc12f2a5, type: 3}
  nameOfStory: Poverty
  hintOfPOI: "The city of Valletta carries the name of Jean Parisot de la Vallette,
    the Grandmaster of the Knights of St John who had ordered the city\u2019s construction.
    The location you need is the residence reserved for the Grandmasters who succeeded
    him - and later became the residence of the British governors during colonial
    rule. "
  solutionOfPOI: "I need to go to the Grandmaster\u2019s Palace, not far from the
    Bibliotheca where I started this journey."
